WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 334: Queen Elizabeth II (Part 1): Accession at Tree Tops, the Coronation of 1953, and the Decolonization of Empire

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:14.880
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series chronicles the opening decades of the longest and most celebrated reign in British history.

4
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:26.640
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary story of the young princess who acceded in a Kenyan treetop and transformed the ancient monarchy for the modern television age.

5
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:47.680
[George] Born on April 21, 1926, at 17 Bruton Street in London's Mayfair, Princess Elizabeth Alexandra Mary, known within her family as Lilibit, was the eldest daughter of the Duke and Duchess of York, when her grandfather King George V fell seriously ill in 1928.

6
00:00:47.680 --> 00:01:03.520
[George] Little Lilibit's precocious, cheerful presence at his bedside was credited with reviving the old king's spirits, while Winston Churchill famously remarked after meeting the two-year-old princess that she possessed an air of authority and reflectiveness astonishing in an infant.

7
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:14.720
[George] Educated at home in constitutional history by Henry Martin, the vice provost of Eton College, Elizabeth grew up with a deep, instinctive reverence for constitutional law and duty.

8
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:23.600
[George] During the London Blitz, she and her younger sister Princess Margaret lived at Windsor Castle, sleeping in subterranean dungeons during air raids.

9
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:34.560
[George] In October 1940, at age 14, Elizabeth made her first radio broadcast on the BBC's Children's Hour, reassuring thousands of evacuated children.

10
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.1000
[George] Determined to participate directly in the national war effort.

11
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.520
[George] Elizabeth badgered her father until he granted her permission to join the Auxiliary Territorial Service in early 1945.

12
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.120
[George] Registered as 2nd subaltern Elizabeth Windsor, No.

13
00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:11.880
[George] 230,873, the future queen wore Greece-stained overalls, learned to strip and rebuild heavy truck engines, drive military ambulances, and change giant wheels, proving her willingness to share the common labour of her generation.

14
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:24.680
[George] On VE Day, May 8, 1945, 19-year-old Elizabeth and her sister Margaret received permission from their parents to slip out of Buckingham Palace incognito to celebrate with the jubilant crowds.

15
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:40.760
[George] Pulling her ATS uniform cap low over her eyes, the future queen danced the Conga through the Ritz Hotel and linked arms with ordinary Londoners in Trafalgar Square, shouting, We want the King, in what she later called one of the most memorable nights of my life.

16
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:57.880
[George] Princess Elizabeth's heart had been one years earlier in July 1939, when, as a thirteen-year-old girl visiting the Royal Naval College at Dartmouth, she was escorted by an 18-year-old naval cadet of dazzling blonde good looks, Prince Philip of Greece and Denmark.

17
00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:11.400
[George] Philip was a descendant of Queen Victoria through his mother, Princess Alice of Battenburg, but his family had been exiled from Greece in turmoil, leaving him impoverished, homeless, and raised by British relatives.

18
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:20.600
[George] During World War II, Philip served with distinction in the Royal Navy, mentioned in dispatches for his heroism during the Battle of Cape Matapan.

19
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:28.040
[George] Elizabeth and Philip exchanged letters throughout the war, and upon his return to London, the young couple fell deeply in love.

20
00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:39.640
[George] Courtiers initially viewed Philip with snobbish skepticism, grumbling that he was rough-mannered, penniless, and had German brothers-in-law, but Elizabeth's determination was unshakable.

21
00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:50.640
[George] In April 1947, during a royal tour of South Africa on her 21st birthday, Elizabeth broadcast a solemn, immortal pledge to the worldwide Commonwealth.

22
00:03:50.640 --> 00:04:00.560
[George] I declare before you all that my whole life, whether it be long or short, shall be devoted to your service and the service of our great imperial family to which we all belong.

23
00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:08.960
[George] On November 20, 1947, Elizabeth married Philip, created Duke of Edinburgh at Westminster Abbey.

24
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:22.160
[George] In post-war Britain, still gripped by severe bread and clothes rationing, the royal wedding was a magical splash of color, with Winston Churchill describing it as a flash of color on the hard road we have to travel.

25
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.440
[George] In early 1952, with King George V.

26
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:35.280
[George] I recovering from lung surgery, Princess Elizabeth and Prince Philip departed on a grand tour of East Africa, Australia, and New Zealand on his behalf.

27
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:51.040
[George] On the afternoon of February 5, 1952, the royal party arrived at Tree Top's Hotel in Kenya, a rustic wooden lodge built high in the branches of an ancient giant fig tree overlooking a natural waterhole in the Aberdare National Park.

28
00:04:51.040 --> 00:05:03.920
[George] Elizabeth spent an enchanted night watching herds of wild elephants, rhinos and baboons gather beneath the tree, filming the magnificent scene with her personal cine-camera and marveling at the African sunrise.

29
00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:11.680
[George] Naturalist Jim Corbett, who served as the royal party's armed bodyguard, famously wrote in the hotel guestbook.

30
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:24.400
[George] For the first time in the history of the world, a young girl climbed into a tree one day a princess, and after having what she described as her most thrilling experience, she climbed down from the tree next day a queen.

31
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.440
[George] Miles away at Sandringham, King George V.

32
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] I died in his sleep in the early morning hours.

33
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:40.680
[George] News of the king's death was relayed through London and Nairobi to Prince Philip, who gently broke the shattering news to his wife beside a trout stream at the nearby Sagana Lodge.

34
00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.680
[George] Twenty-five-year-old Elizabeth displayed extraordinary composure.

35
00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:54.200
[George] When her private secretary Martin Charteris asked what regnal name she wished to assume, she replied simply, My own name, of course, Elizabeth.

36
00:05:54.200 --> 00:06:01.480
[George] She immediately began writing letters of apology to the leaders of Australia and New Zealand for cancelling the remainder of her tour.

37
00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:15.800
[George] Landing at London Airport dressed in morning black, the young Queen was met at the aircraft steps by Prime Minister Winston Churchill, Clement Attlee, and Anthony Eden, bearing the weight of the British crown upon her youthful shoulders.

38
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.080
[George] Queen Elizabeth I.

39
00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:14.640
[George] The United Kingdom watched the coronation live on television, crowding around tiny, nine-inch black and white screens in living rooms, village halls and church basements, while tens of millions more listened on the radio across six continents.

40
00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:24.800
[George] Despite unseasonal summer rain, three million people lined the London streets as the glittering gold state coach carried the 27-year-old Queen to Westminster Abbey.

41
00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.320
[George] Inside the abbey.

42
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:43.840
[George] The holy moment of anointing, when the Archbishop of Canterbury touched consecrated oil onto the Queen's hands, breast and head, was shielded from the cameras beneath a golden canopy of cloth of gold, preserving the ancient, mystical spiritual covenant between the sovereign and God.

43
00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:58.960
[George] That very morning, news arrived that a British expedition led by John Hunt, with Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay, had reached the summit of Mount Everest, sparking euphoric national celebration that a glorious new Elizabethan age had dawned.

44
00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:04.720
[George] In the weeks leading up to the ceremony, the young Queen had practiced wearing the nearly five-pound St.

45
00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:21.600
[George] Edward's crown during her daily desk work, wearing it while taking tea and reviewing state papers so that her neck muscles would not falter beneath the sacred weight during the three-hour coronation ritual, demonstrating the meticulous professionalism that would define her seven-decade reign.

46
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.200
[George] Immediately following her coronation, Queen Elizabeth I.

47
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:54.880
[George] In Australia, an astounding three-quarters of the entire continent's population turned out to see their young queen in person, an outpouring of imperial warmth never equaled in history.

48
00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:01.160
[George] Yet this glittering imperial harmony was soon shattered by the geopolitical convulsions of the Cold War.

49
00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:17.080
[George] In October 1956, Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalized the Suez Canal, prompting British Prime Minister Sir Anthony Eden to orchestrate a secret, disastrous Anglo-French-Israeli military invasion to seize back the canal.

50
00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:20.520
[George] The Suez Crisis was an unmitigated disaster.

51
00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:25.800
[George] Furious diplomatic and financial pressure from United States President Dwight D.

52
00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:36.120
[George] Eisenhower forced Britain into a humiliating military retreat, shattering Britain's illusion of being an independent global superpower and forcing Eden's resignation.

53
00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:54.600
[George] Through this humiliating national crisis, Queen Elizabeth maintained scrupulous constitutional neutrality, advising her ministers in private while recognizing that Britain's future lay not in military empire, but in the voluntary, egalitarian partnership of the expanding Commonwealth of Nations.

54
00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:05.960
[George] The 1960s witnessed the rapid, breathtaking dissolution of the British Empire as dozens of former colonies across Africa, the Caribbean, and Asia gained full independence.

55
00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:26.520
[George] When Prime Minister Harold Macmillan delivered his famous, wind of change speech to the South African Parliament in February 1960, warning that African national consciousness was an unstoppable force, Queen Elizabeth embraced decolonization not with bitter imperial regret, but with visionary enthusiasm for the modern commonwealth.

56
00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:39.800
[George] In November 1961, despite grave security warnings from the British cabinet that Marxist President Quayme Nkrumah might be targeted for assassination, the Queen courageously proceeded with a state visit to Ghana.

57
00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:58.000
[George] At a state ball in Accra, Elizabeth famously danced the Foxtrot with President Nkrumah, a radiant, electrifying gesture of multiracial partnership that destroyed apartheid propaganda, delighted African leaders, and cemented Ghana's decision to remain within the Commonwealth family.

58
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:11.360
[George] Throughout the 1960s, as Ghana, Nigeria, Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, Jamaica, and Trinidad gained their independence, Elizabeth embraced each newly independent leader as an equal head of state.

59
00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:19.520
[George] Transforming the Commonwealth from an imperial relic into a unique, dynamic international forum representing a third of humanity.

60
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:27.920
[George] In 1977, Great Britain celebrated Queen Elizabeth I's silver jubilee, marking 25 years on the throne.

61
00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:46.000
[George] The milestone occurred against a bleak backdrop of economic stagflation, three-day working weeks, soaring inflation, industrial strikes, and violent sectarian conflict in Northern Ireland, leading skeptical commentators to predict that the Jubilee would be a sullen, embarrassing failure.

62
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.920
[George] Instead, the silver jubilee unleashed an astonishing wave of genuine national celebration.

63
00:11:51.920 --> 00:12:06.560
[George] Over one million people packed the mall to cheer the Queen on Jubilee Day, while more than 12,000 street parties transformed working class neighborhoods across London, Manchester, and Glasgow into sea of red, white, and blue bunting.

64
00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:13.680
[George] The Queen and Prince Philip travelled 12,000 miles across Britain, meeting over a million citizens in person.

65
00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:23.280
[George] Even the rebellious counterculture of the punk explosion, epitomized by the Sex Pistols' satirical single, God Save the Queen, could not diminish the deep.

66
00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:30.680
[George] Affectionate respect ordinary citizens felt for the steadfast woman who had served them without complaint for a quarter of a century.

67
00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:35.800
[George] At a Guild Hall luncheon in London, Elizabeth reaffirmed her lifelong dedication.

68
00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:43.320
[George] When I was twenty one, I pledged my life to the service of our people, and I asked for God's help to make good that vow.

69
00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:51.880
[George] Although that vow was made in my salad days, when I was green in judgment, I do not regret nor take back one single word.

70
00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:16.440
[Rebecca] Join us next time for the grand finale of our Kings and Queens of England series the fairy tale wedding of Prince Charles and Lady Diana Spencer, the trials of the Annis Horribilis, 70 magnificent years of service culminating in the Platinum Jubilee, and the accession of King Charles III.

71
00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:23.640
